      "claim": "A trained non-expert panel using an explicit checklist produces more reliable judgments than a single intuition-reliant expert because the checklist externalizes the cognitive operations that constitute expert competence into a distributed, executable procedure, eliminating the variance introduced by expert stochastic retrieval and attention fluctuations.",
      "mechanism": "Expert intuition is a high-variance stochastic retrieval process over a large internal knowledge base, where a non-expert panel following a checklist is a deterministic serial application of circumscribed rules. The checklist works not by approximating the expert but by decomposing the judgment task into discrete propositions (P) that an expert can verify reliably. Thus the checklist externalizes the true inferential structure of the task and processes it via parallel independent verification by non-experts, reducing the noise that dominates expert error.",
      "counterconditions": [
        "If the task requires identifying which factors are relevant before applying logic to them (e.g., diagnosing a rare disease from a vague complaint), the checklist cannot contain the expertise needed, and the panel will apply the right steps to the wrong inputs, performing worse than the expert.",
        "If the checklist does not cover all relevant cases or has incomplete rules, the panel will systematically miss edge cases that an expert would catch intuitively.",
        "If the task outcome depends on factors that cannot be adequately captured in a discrete propositional checklist, the panel will underperform."
      ],
