      "claim": "The panel of non-experts using a checklist will, on average, be more reliable than a single expert relying on intuition, because the panel leverages a Condorcet-style aggregation mechanism: even if each non-expert's checklist-driven judgment is individually noisier than the expert's intuition, independent application of the same checklist across multiple panelists cancels random errors through majority aggregation.",
      "mechanism": "Each non-expert independently applies the checklist, which encodes the most discriminative features of the problem. Their errors are largely independent (driven by individual lapses in rule execution, not shared systematic blind spots). When aggregated, these independent errors cancel. The expert's intuition, by contrast, is a single sample from a stochastic internal process; it lacks the aggregation benefit. Therefore, if the checklist encodes the task's discriminative features even modestly well, the panel's aggregated judgment converges toward the correct answer with higher probability than the expert's single sample.",
      "counterconditions": [
